# Twitter / X — 2010

**The year the life the rest of this wiki describes actually starts.** In

fifty-three days Dan passes a Pro Tools certification exam, announces Brooklyn

eleven minutes later, gets his first tattoo, leaves Florida for good, and moves

into Williamsburg with Alexis. By March he has a New York studio job and a work

email address. By December he is quitting tobacco, posting Galileo at his

family, and complaining about the rent.

It also contains — unlabelled, and almost certainly without meaning to — the

single most consequential date in the corpus. The wiki records **17 February

2010** as day zero of a Suboxone regimen that has run without a break ever

since. There are three tweets on that date. The third one is *"this is the most

stressful day/decision of my life."*

## 17 February 2010, and a date that survives its own reasoning

The day has three entries. At 13:32 UTC, *"off to Williamsburg."* At 16:44,

*"@alexisarmel welcome home"* with a photo. Then at **20:07 UTC — 15:07 New

York time**:

> *"this is the most stressful day/decision of my life. nothing is making it

> easier. ugh laying down for a little bit."*

The next morning he is *"On the train back to PA... can't wait to get home and

see babygirl"*, and posts *"smart has plans, stupid has stories."* Two days

later: *"planning the next 3 years of my life all day in muh head"*, *"laying

down because thats the only thing i cant mess up"*, and *"put my life on a

treadmill. shape up or bust."*

**What that decision was, he never says.** The obvious candidate is ruled out

by the record: the Brooklyn move had been settled twenty-two days earlier, on

26 January, and every mention of it before and after this day is unambiguously

good news — *"Brooklyn here we come!!"*, *"moving to brooklyn in 9 days.

out-fucking-standing."* Nobody calls a decision already made and celebrated the

most stressful of their life three weeks later. What remains is a decision he

was unwilling to name in public, taken on the exact day the wiki dates the

start of sixteen years of opioid maintenance, in the same week his own account

describes as the last of active use.

**This is stated as a dated alignment, not as a proof, and the reason to be

strict about that is that the date's existing provenance turns out to be

unsound.** [[wiki/health/chemical-architecture]] carries 17 February 2010 as

"day-zero pinned". Its origin is `raw/self/dox-scan/Gemini-_58.txt`: a model,

reading email logs, told Dan that *"on Thursday, February 18, you and your dad

boarded an Amtrak train... bound for Penn Station"*, and Dan replied:

> *"NO no - if the train left on feb 18 that means that the first day i began

> my successful suboxone streak was feb 17, 2010."*

So the date is not remembered. It is **computed** — the day before a train —

and the anchor is a model's reconstruction rather than his own recall.

> **CONTRADICTION [2026-09-03]:** the anchor is misread. The 18 February train

> was the **return** leg, not the outbound one. On 17 February Dan is already

> in New York (*"off to Williamsburg"*), and on the 18th he is *"On the train

> back to PA."* The apartment hunt the Gemini session places on that trip

> matches 17 February, not the 18th. The derivation "the train left on the

> 18th, so day zero was the 17th" therefore rests on a leg pointing the wrong

> way, and **the arithmetic that produced the wiki's most-cited date does not

> hold.**

>

> The date itself survives anyway, on better evidence than the reasoning that

> produced it: 17 February is the day carrying *"the most stressful

> day/decision of my life"*, contemporaneously, in his own words, at a moment

> when nothing else in his life was going badly. **The conclusion is unchanged

> and its support is now different, which is worth recording precisely because

> a claim that changes footing without anybody noticing is the kind this

> corpus is built to catch.**

Two smaller corrections fall out of the same passage. The Gemini session states

that the February trip *"coincided with your graduation from Full Sail"* —

a third date for an event the wiki elsewhere puts in August 2009 and this

corpus puts no earlier than late December 2009 ([[wiki/self/twitter/2009]]). And

it has him arriving in New York to stay on 18 February, when the record has him

leaving Orlando on **28 February** (*"peace out, florida. it's been real."*),

which is the date [[wiki/timeline/periods/full-sail-2008-2010]] already gives.

That page's Florida-departure date is now corroborated by a same-day tweet.

## Fifty-three days: certification to Brooklyn

The move is documented to the hour and its trigger is a credential, not a plan.

On **25 January**: *"ughhhhhhhhhhh missed my 210m by 3 questions."* On the

**26th**, *"oh me oh my, going to take my 210m"*, then *"EAT UR HEART OUT BITCH

IM PRO TOOLS CERTIFIED"*, and eleven minutes after that, *"Brooklyn here we

come!!"* He had been saying *"can't wait to get to NYC ughhhhhhh"* since the

23rd; the exam is what converted the wish into a plan, and he undercuts it the

same day — *"it's sort of ironic how i finish my pro tools certifications and

go directly to Logic."*

Then: 20 February, *"moving to brooklyn in 9 days"*; 25 February, a *"welcome

to BK mix"* made for Alexis, *"ft. ODB, GZA, BIG, jigga, vampy wknd, grizbear

& TVotr"*; 27 February, *"last day in errrlando...spending it how I always

imagined: hangin in a u-haul store"*; 28 February, *"peace out, florida."*

## The first job, and the first work email

On **24 March**: *"has a broken foot and a new work email address. big day.

update your contacts: dan@ishlab.com."* That is the corpus's dated start for

the Ishlab period, and the year fills in what the job actually was — *"at

work/////"* on a weekday, *"2 more hours and my weekend begins"*, a colleague's

freestyle he could not return (*"i might have made it 2 bars in. urbanfail"*,

19 October), three days of *"tag analysis"* that he hated (*"all i want is to

listen to a whole song. instead, i get a million 10 sec clips"*, 15 October),

*"reggaeton monday @ ishlab"*, *"boss leaves for tour tomorrow"*, and

*"watching 'the week in rap' filming at the studio. i hate everything"*

(9 December).

The tone is worth keeping: this is the music-industry job he moved to a city

for, and within nine months he is describing it as tagging ten-second clips

and hating everything. On **21 December** — *"Y U NO PAY ME ?"*

## The account publishes its own geography

From late March the feed fills with Foursquare check-ins, and they amount to a

**first-party, self-published location record** for a period the corpus

otherwise reconstructs: Brooklyn Gourmet Deli at 313 Bedford Ave, the Marcy Ave

J/M/Z platform, the J train at Flushing St, Brooklyn Bridge Park, Walgreens at

Union and Meserole, the C-Town at South 1st and Havemeyer, Grimaldi's, Ako on

Bedford, Peter Luger, and Electric Zoo on Randall's Island. He unlocked the

Foursquare "Adventurer" and "Swarm" badges in the same year.

This is a rare thing in the corpus and should be treated as such: a dated,

addressed, voluntarily published trail, from someone whose later record is

mostly reconstructed from other people's messages. It corroborates

[[wiki/places/424-bedford-ave]] from the outside — the check-ins cluster on the

blocks around it — and it is the reason the 2010 geography is better evidenced

than the 2013 geography, despite 2013 having more tweets.

## Two musical migrations, running at the same time

The year is usually read as the indie year, and it is: Grizzly Bear, Vampire

Weekend (*"vampy weekend did 124k?! hooooly shit"*), TV on the Radio, Yeasayer,

Animal Collective, Beach House, Titus Andronicus, Arcade Fire's *The Suburbs*

(*"oh my gaawd"*, 31 July), Radiohead (*"i cant decide between OKC or KIDA"*).

But the electronic material is already running beside it, not after it: Diplo

at R Bar in March and again at Electric Zoo in September (*"@diplo slayed

electric zoo. what was the track you finished with?"*), Die Antwoord's Ninja

spotted in the street, HARD NYC, A-Trak, Duck Sauce, Crystal Castles, Steve

Aoki, and on **27 March** *"learning to warp in ableton"* — a specific

production technique, named. By **14 September** he is publishing output:

*"here's a super quick dubstep mix I did the other day."*

So the guitar-to-bass migration that [[wiki/mind/synthesis/millennial-digital-witness]]

locates in the 2012–2014 likes data is **already underway in 2010**, with both

vocabularies live in the same months. What the likes data may be dating is when

one displaced the other, not when the second one started.

## The atheism goes public, and it goes public in Pennsylvania

`CONTEXT_CORE_EXPANDED` dates aggressive atheism to 2007. 2010 is where it

first appears in his public writing, and the timing is not random — it clusters

on trips home. **26 November**, from Carmichaels the day after Thanksgiving

dinner, he posts a Galileo quotation about a God who *"endowed us with sense,

reason, and intellect"* and cannot have *"intended us to forgo their use."* On

**25 December**: *"christmas...we should nail whoever invented this shit to a

fucking cross."* On **27 December**: *"The most dominant idol for christianity

is the cross, do you really think if Jesus comes back he's going to want to see

a fucking cross?!?!"*

Underneath it the same year runs a quieter intellectual diet in the same

direction — the *Skeptics' Guide to the Universe* podcast (26 October), Dan

Gilbert on synthetic happiness (27 September), and on **19 December**,

unprompted, *"we're all tiny, ignorant, insignificant bits of carbon."*

The pattern is worth naming because it recurs: the ideological statements are

made **at** the household, from inside it, during obligatory visits. On

24 November, arriving: *"objective for the week- avoid any/all hometowners."*

## 13 December: a dependency solved by changing the delivery system

> *"3 days after moving to an e cig, i'm (un) hooked. today is day 1 with no

> tobacco. technology is fuggin ~crazy~"*

Then *"25 hours tobacco free. i should have done this years ago :("* on the

14th, and on the 15th *"man, so I can like...taste stuff now. crazy life

stuff."*

Taken alone this is a dated smoking-cessation event, and `ADDICTION_PROFILE`

lists nicotine as one of the three standing chemical dependencies. Taken

alongside the rest of the year it is the **second** dependency in ten months

addressed the same way: not by stopping the molecule but by **replacing the

delivery system with a controlled one and keeping the molecule**. That is what

a maintenance dose is, and it is what an e-cigarette is.

The honest limit on that observation: he says nothing connecting the two, the

February decision is unnamed, and one structural rhyme between two events is a

pattern with n=2. It is recorded here because the corpus's account of how Dan

handles compulsion is currently built on the opioid case alone, and a second

instance — trivial, cheerful, ten months later, in an entirely different

substance — is the sort of thing that either replicates later or does not.

## The body takes damage twice

**24 March**: *"has a broken foot."* **27 October**: *"broken wrist AND

hiccups?! I'd say today was a smashing success"*, with the cause given the next

day as a *"vintage 12 speed."* Two fractures in seven months, both apparently

from riding a bicycle across Brooklyn in all weather — *"tried to be hardcore

and bike through the rain. now i get 8 hours in soaked jeans"* (25 August). The

broken foot and the new job land on the same day and he files them as one item.

## The account's own age, settled

[[wiki/self/twitter/2008]] says the creation date *"was not retrievable"* and

that 24 September 2008 is an inference from the earliest surviving tweet. 2010

closes that gap with two contemporaneous third-party counters he posted

himself:

| Date | Tweet | Implied creation |

|---|---|---|

| 2010-08-24 | *"I joined twitter 699 days ago"* (`whendidyoujointwitter.com`) | 2008-09-24 |

| 2010-12-09 | *"I joined Twitter on the 23rd of September 2008, making my account 807 days old"* (`twuration.com`) | 2008-09-23 |

The second names the date outright. The one-day gap between them is what a

timezone boundary looks like, and it resolves cleanly: the first surviving

tweet is 2008-09-24 04:52 UTC, which is **00:52 on 24 September in New York** —

so an account created on the evening of the 23rd local time produces a first

tweet dated the 24th in UTC, and 2022's `#MyTwitterAnniversary` post on

24 September is consistent with either. **The best-supported reading is

23 September 2008, local time**, with 24 September being the UTC-side artefact.

He also posted *"proud member of the 'twitter since 08 club'"* on 24 August

2010, which independently rules out any later year.

## The relationship, in public and under attack

Alexis is in the replies constantly and the register is unguarded — *"u make me

feel like luckiest dude evar. i love youuu (from 2 feet away on the couch)"*

(22 January), *"i only feel okay when you're around"* (23 January), *"things

are perfect<3 i've never really meant that before"* (30 January), *"amazing

what a difference one person can make in your life. i've never felt this happy

and motivated before. it's always been you"* (26 February), and by late April

the hashtag *#marriedlife*. The tattoo — her initial, his first — is on

23–24 January, sixteen minutes from *"getting my 'a' tattoo on monday"* to

*"scratch that going to get it NOW!"*

On **22 March** something breaks the surface:

> *"i think it's pathetic how badly everyone wants to see us react. just

> because your life is miserable, doesn't mean we can't be happy."*

> *"also- this life is full of the fucking win, if you found what i have...you

> wouldn't give it up either."*

He does not say who "everyone" is. It is the year's only defensive post about

the relationship and it is addressed to an audience that was evidently

watching. Worth carrying forward: the relationship is contested from outside

within four months of the move, five years before it ends.

## What would change this reading

If a pharmacy or clinic record surfaces with a Suboxone induction date other

than 17 February 2010, the alignment above collapses into a coincidence and the

February decision needs a different referent — the record would then contain a

"most stressful decision of my life" with nothing attached to it, which is

itself worth knowing. If Ishlab employment records give a start date before

24 March, the work-email tweet is a change of address rather than a first day.

And if the missing months of 2010 arrive from a complete export, everything

here about *when* in the year something clusters is provisional: this page,

like 2009, is a fetch and not a census.

